# /etc/pam.d/greetd — an existence marker, not a policy.
#
# greetd refuses to start unless /etc/pam.d/<service> or /usr/lib/pam.d/<service>
# exists for its configured service name (greetd/src/server.rs pam_service_exists,
# a plain Path::exists on both prefixes). The check is the only thing that reads
# this path: LeandrOS has no PAM module stack, and authentication is implemented
# directly against /etc/shadow by ports/greetd/pam-leandros, which never opens
# /etc/pam.d at all.
#
# So the content below is documentation. Editing it changes nothing; deleting the
# file stops greetd from starting.
#
# For reference, the stack this shim stands in for on a real distribution:
#   auth       required   pam_unix.so
#   account    required   pam_unix.so
#   session    required   pam_unix.so
